      "author": "NASA - National Aeronautics and Space Administration",
      "body": "That's our jam \ud83c\udfbc\n \nAstronomers used our Chandra X-ray Observatory to find out that this radio wave formation, known as the \"Treble Clef\" thanks to its distinctive shape, was likely caused by an outburst from a supermassive black hole, followed by a collision of two galaxy clusters.",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.facebook.com/100044561550831/posts/1664477875047582",
      "id": "post:1664477875047582",
      "links": [],
      "media": [
        {
          "description": "A composite image of a radio wave formation that looks a lot like a treble clef musical symbol, hovering in the midst of two colliding galaxy clusters. There are many specks of light set against the blackness of space; those are galaxies and stars. At the center of the image is a large, oval, blue-purple cloud with ragged edges. In the middle of the purple field, tilting slightly to our left, is what appears to be a giant treble clef, loosely drawn with a neon pink marker. Like the musical notation for which it's named, the formation has a strong vertical stroke with a continuous, fluid line swooping back and forth as it moves from top to bottom.",
          "type": "image",
          "url": "https://scontent-mia5-2.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.30808-6/857745998_1664477865047583_7948459830471427458_n.jpg"
        }
